    "body": "DHL Supply Chain is testing warehouse robots that can decide how to respond when conditions change. The company is also rolling out AI software in live warehouses to help site managers coordinate workers, robots, and other equipment as orders come in.\nWhat’s Related\nDHL announced the moves earlier this week. The software is moving into day-to-day use after an initial test, while the adaptive robots are still in pilot programs.\n“Our immediate priority is to continue scaling our proven robotics across more sites while moving the next generation of adaptive AI-powered robotics from pilots into live operations,” said Tim Tetzlaff, DHL Supply Chain’s global head of digital transformation.\nDHL says conventional warehouse robots typically follow set routes or instructions. The robots it’s testing are given a task to complete and can use information about their surroundings to decide how to proceed if conditions differ from what they expected. The company did not specify which jobs the adaptive robots are being tested on or report how they performed in the pilots.\nRELATED STORIES: Six Trends Changing How Robots Work in Warehouses | Before You Buy Warehouse Robots, Know What You’re Trying to Fix\nHow it comes together\nDHL calls the software AI orchestration. It acts as a control center, pulling together data from warehouse systems so site managers can adjust how they use workers and equipment as demand changes.\nIn one workflow DHL is testing, the software connects mobile robots and automated packing equipment with the warehouse management system , which tracks orders and directs work. DHL says the setup uses incoming order data to shift resources, which can help warehouses process more orders. It did not say how much more.\nRobots at scale\nDHL says it has deployed more than 9,000 robots worldwide. Its robots have made more than 1.5 billion picks , and Boston Dynamics’ Stretch robots have unloaded 10 million cases at DHL sites.\nThe company has used advanced analytics and AI tools more than 1,400 times, including more than 500 deployments in 2026. It also logged more than 2.5 million minutes of interactions with conversational AI tools. DHL says more than 95% of its warehouses worldwide already use advanced digital tools.\nWhere warehouse workers fit in\nDHL says robots can handle repetitive, physically demanding work, while employees use their judgment to handle situations that fall outside the usual routine.\n“The most successful supply chains will not be fully automated,” Tetzlaff said. “They will combine the speed and analytical capabilities of technology with the experience and judgment of people.”",
